  "worked_solution": "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection. \n\n- First, we solve $7x>-7\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & >-7 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & >\\frac{-7}{7} \\\\ x & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/9b902e22e6808cf01993494e199b9fcbd184d084e385ef94cb9a1953c00abb7c.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $3x<3\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & <3 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & >\\frac{3}{3} \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/ca26af3420f15a115c44ddb234afe4226e850799b5f91625faaee4c4fad37a4a.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194110/assets/12b3a4dedd88b66e7c06812b83c3195e889bdf22d40e45432e8569aaf5764047.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThus, the final solution is $-1<x<1.$",
